# Line endings, pinned per file type, for every checkout on every system.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true, which checks text files out
# with CRLF line endings. bash then reads the \r as part of every command:
#
#   set: pipefail\r: invalid option name
#   $'\r': command not found
#
# on line 1 of every script, before anything can explain itself. Without this
# file, a Windows clone of this repository could not run a single script. These
# rules take precedence over autocrlf, so a Windows checkout is byte-identical to
# a Linux or macOS one.
#
# The upstream checkouts under */upstream are separate repositories with their
# own .gitattributes and are deliberately left to them: tdesktop, for one, wants
# CRLF .bat files on Windows, and the Python in scripts/ reads and writes in text
# mode, which keeps whatever convention a checkout already uses.
* text=auto eol=lf

# cmd.exe mis-parses LF-only batch files (labels and goto break at buffer
# boundaries); PowerShell reads either, and CRLF is what Windows editors write.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never converted, never diffed as text.
*.png      binary
*.jpg      binary
*.webp     binary
*.jks      binary
*.keystore binary
*.apk      binary
*.aab      binary
